```example
>>> #set page(width: 140pt)
$ a + cancel(x, length: #200%)
    - cancel(x, length: #200%) $
```¨invertedÚ+Whether the cancel line should be inverted (flipped along the y-axis).
For the default angle setting, inverted means the cancel line points to
the top left instead of top right.

```example
>>> #set page(width: 140pt)
$ (a cancel((b + c), inverted: #true)) /
    cancel(b + c, inverted: #true) $
```¥crossÙ³Whether two opposing cancel lines should be drawn, forming a cross over
the element. Overrides `inverted`.

```example
>>> #set page(width: 140pt)
$ cancel(Pi, cross: #true) $
```ÚHow much to rotate the cancel line.

- If given an angle, the line is rotated by that angle clockwise with
  respect to the y-axis.
- If `{auto}`, the line assumes the default angle; that is, along the
  rising diagonal of the content box.
- If given a function `angle => angle`, the line is rotated, with
  respect to the y-axis, by the angle returned by that function. The
  function receives the default angle as its input.

```example
>>> #set page(width: 140pt)
$ cancel(Pi)
  cancel(Pi, angle: #0deg)
  cancel(Pi, angle: #45deg)
  cancel(Pi, angle: #90deg)
  cancel(1/(1+x), angle: #(a => a + 45deg))
  cancel(1/(1+x), angle: #(a => a + 90deg)) $
```Ù¶How to @stroke[stroke] the cancel line.

```example
>>> #set page(width: 140pt)
$ cancel(
  sum x,
  stroke: #(
    paint: red,
    thickness: 1.5pt,
    dash: "dashed",
  ),
) $
```¥cases¥delimÚThe delimiter to use.

Can be a single character specifying the left delimiter, in which case
the right delimiter is inferred. Otherwise, can be an array containing a
left and a right delimiter.

```example
#set math.cases(delim: "[")
$ x = cases(1, 2) $
```§reverseÙuWhether the direction of cases should be reversed.

```example
#set math.cases(reverse: true)
$ cases(1, 2) = x $
```£gapÙWThe gap between branches.

```example
#set math.cases(gap: 1em)
$ x = cases(1, 2) $
```¨childrenÙ%The branches of the case distinction.¤cbor¦sourceÙ(A path to a CBOR file or raw CBOR bytes.«cbor.encode´Value to be encoded.¦circleÙJThe circle's radius. This is mutually exclusive with `width` and
`height`.ÙšThe circle's width. This is mutually exclusive with `radius` and
`height`.

In contrast to `radius`, this can be relative to the parent container's
width.Ù›The circle's height. This is mutually exclusive with `radius` and
`width`.

In contrast to `radius`, this can be relative to the parent container's
